<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title><%= vuln.name %> - Vuln-World</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #f9fafb; color: #374151; line-height: 1.6; }
    header { background: #1f2937; color: #fff; padding: 15px 30px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    header a { color: #9ca3af; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; }
    header a:hover { color: white; }
    .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 40px auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    h1 { margin-top: 0; color: #111827; }
    h2 { color: #1f2937; border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-top: 40px; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 18px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 8px; background: #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-right: 10px; color: #4b5563; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: bold; margin: 20px 0; }
    .btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
    pre { background: #1f2937; color: #f8f8f2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; }
    .vuln-code { border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; }
    .sec-code { border-left: 4px solid #10b981; }
    .diagram { background: #f3f4f6; padding: 20px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
    ul { padding-left: 20px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/vulnerabilities">← Back to Vulnerabilities</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1><%= vuln.name %></h1>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.category %></span>
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.difficulty %></span>
    </div>
    
    <a href="/labs/<%= vuln.lab %>?challenge=<%= vuln.id %>" class="btn">Start Lab (<%= vuln.lab %>)</a>
    
    <h2>What Is It?</h2>
    <p>Path Traversal (also known as Directory Traversal) is a vulnerability that allows an attacker to read arbitrary files on the server that is running an application. This might include application code, data, credentials for back-end systems, and sensitive operating system files.</p>

    <h2>In Simple Words</h2>
    <p>Imagine telling a librarian to get a book from the "Public" shelf, but instead you tell them to go "up one floor, down the hall, and into the manager's office" to grab a confidential file. The librarian blindly follows your directions and gives you the file.</p>

    <h2>Where Does It Appear?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>File download or viewing features (e.g., <code>?file=report.pdf</code>)</li>
      <li>Image loading endpoints</li>
      <li>Template inclusion mechanisms</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Does It Occur?</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
Request: GET /download?file=../../../etc/passwd

[Web Server] 
  |__ /var/www/html/downloads/
         |__ (resolves ../../../etc/passwd)
  |__ /etc/passwd <--- Fetched!
    </div>

    <h2>HTTP Example</h2>
    <pre>
GET /image?filename=../../../etc/passwd HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
    </pre>

    <h2>Burp Suite Investigation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Identify parameters that seem to reference file names.</li>
      <li>Send the request to Burp Repeater.</li>
      <li>Modify the parameter value by prepending <code>../</code> sequences (e.g., <code>../../../../etc/passwd</code> or <code>..%2f..%2f..%2fwindows\win.ini</code>).</li>
      <li>Check the response for the contents of the sensitive file.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How Developers Accidentally Create It</h2>
    <p>Vulnerable Code:</p>
    <pre class="vuln-code">
app.get('/download', (req, res) => {
  const file = req.query.file;
  // Concatenating without validation
  const filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'public_files', file);
  res.sendFile(filePath);
});
    </pre>
    <p>Secure Code:</p>
    <pre class="sec-code">
app.get('/download', (req, res) => {
  const file = req.query.file;
  const baseDir = path.join(__dirname, 'public_files');
  const filePath = path.join(baseDir, file);
  
  // Verify the resolved path starts with the base directory
  if (!filePath.startsWith(baseDir)) {
    return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
  }
  res.sendFile(filePath);
});
    </pre>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Information Disclosure (reading source code, configuration files, passwords).</li>
      <li>Potentially Remote Code Execution if combined with file upload (writing to sensitive directories).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Find It in Real Applications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Look for parameters containing `.ext` like `.pdf`, `.png`, or words like `file`, `path`, `doc`.</li>
      <li>Use automated scanners or fuzzers with wordlists containing traversal payloads.</li>
      <li>Try URL encoding (`%2e%2e%2f`) or double URL encoding to bypass basic WAFs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <p>Avoid passing user-supplied input to filesystem APIs altogether if possible (use database IDs instead). If necessary, validate the input against a strict allowlist of file names. Ensure that after resolving the absolute path, the path strictly begins with the expected base directory.</p>

    <h2>Secure Design Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use indirect object references (like UUIDs) instead of file names.</li>
      <li>Always validate the canonicalized, absolute path.</li>
      <li>Run the web server with least privilege permissions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Lab Investigation Guide</h2>
    <p>In this lab, there's a feature to download a public file. Observe how the filename is passed in the URL. Your goal is to navigate outside the intended public folder to read a secret file located in the parent directory.</p>

    <h2>Post-Lab Explanation</h2>
    <p>The application took the filename parameter and concatenated it with the base directory without checking the final path. By using <code>../</code> in the filename parameter, you instructed the operating system to traverse up one directory level, breaking out of the safe folder and accessing the hidden secret file.</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
